STEP 3: MLOps LIFECYCLE

The MLOps lifecycle consists of the following stages:

1. Data Collection and Data Engineering
2. Data Validation and Data Versioning
3. Feature Engineering and Feature Store Management
4. Model Training and Experimentation
5. Model Evaluation and Validation
6. Model Packaging and Versioning
7. Continuous Integration and Continuous Deployment
   (CI/CD) of ML Pipelines
8. Model Deployment and Serving
9. Monitoring, Logging and Retraining

--------------------------------------------------

STEP 4: KEY COMPONENTS OF MLOps

Component                  Purpose
------------------------------------------------------------
Version Control (Git)      Tracks changes to source code
                           and configuration.

Data/Model Versioning      Tracks changes to datasets and
(DVC)                      trained models.

Experiment Tracking        Logs parameters, metrics and
(MLflow)                   artifacts of ML runs.

Feature Store (Feast)      Central repository of reusable,
                           consistent features.

CI/CD                      Automates build, test and release
(GitHub Actions/GitLab CI) of ML pipelines.

Containerization           Packages the model and its
(Docker)                   dependencies for deployment.

Serving                    Exposes the trained model as
(Flask/FastAPI)             a REST API.

Monitoring                 Tracks model performance, data
                           drift and system health in
                           production.

--------------------------------------------------

STEP 5: CHALLENGES IN PRODUCTION MACHINE LEARNING SYSTEMS

1. Data Drift and Concept Drift:
   Changes in data patterns can cause model performance
   degradation over time.

2. Reproducibility:
   Reproducing experiments across different environments
   can be difficult.

3. Version Management:
   Managing dependencies between data, code and model
   versions is challenging.

4. Scalability:
   Training and serving infrastructure must handle
   increasing workloads.

5. Monitoring:
   Monitoring model performance and detecting silent
   failures in production is difficult.

6. Team Coordination:
   Coordination between data scientists, ML engineers
   and operations teams is necessary.

